etails about you'll find out eventually  I assume but one of the things we were  doing as part of that project is we were  kind of trying to characterize some just  like just like what group level stuff  looks like in our literature generally  and one of the things we were looking at  is something called Cohen's D Z so we've  talked about Cohen's D effect sizes on  on this podcast before we've written  about them on the website and a Cohen's  D effect size is essentially how large  the average change is relative to a  pre-training standard deviation so you  know if you're dealing with a group of  people that bench presses a hundred plus  or minus 20 pounds and the the mean  increase in bench press strength in a  study is 10 pounds then the Cohen's D  effect size is that 10 pound change  divided by the 20 pound standard  deviation or 0.5 so there is a similar  metric called Cohen's D Z which is  sometimes called a signal to noise  effect size where that is the change the  mean change divided by the standard  deviation of the change so in that same  example if they bench 100 plus or minus  20 pounds pre-training and their bench  presses increase by 10 plus or minus 5  pounds during training the Cohen's D  effect size is 0.5 the Cohen's D Z  effect size is the 10 pound mean change  divided by the 5 pound standard  deviation of the change or 2 so that  would be the Cohen's D Z and so one of  the things we wanted to see is kind of  within resistance training literature  how variable do gains and strength tend  to be so we found like a be some study  and unfortunately a minority of the  studies in our field actually report  Cohen's D Z effect sizes or just report  the variability in the change of  strength in the first place which is  pretty unfortunate but of those like 80  some studies about 20 of them are so  reported enough information to calculate  Cohen's DZ effect sizes and we found  that the average Cohen's D Z was about  1.6 and that was remarkably homogeneous  s